WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.680
<v Anna>It's 8:42 in the morning.

00:00:02.920 --> 00:00:05.760
<v Anna>Checkout fails across every region at once.

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:08.440
<v Anna>Customers can't complete a purchase anywhere.

00:00:08.869 --> 00:00:10.994
<v Greg>Revenue stops, and it stops visibly.

00:00:11.237 --> 00:00:13.002
<v Greg>The pressure arrives within minutes.

00:00:13.429 --> 00:00:15.509
<v Anna>Alerts fire from every layer.

00:00:15.989 --> 00:00:20.309
<v Anna>Database, network and application teams all need to be mobilised,

00:00:20.429 --> 00:00:22.629
<v Anna>and none of them can diagnose it alone.

00:00:22.978 --> 00:00:25.033
<v Greg>That's what makes it a major incident.

00:00:25.416 --> 00:00:28.470
<v Greg>The response needs coordination that nobody has time to invent

00:00:28.504 --> 00:00:29.294
<v Greg>on the spot.

00:00:29.705 --> 00:00:32.905
<v Anna>So a P1 response needs a cadence before it needs

00:00:32.985 --> 00:00:36.265
<v Anna>a perfect diagnosis. Declare the incident.

00:00:36.505 --> 00:00:42.505
<v Anna>Open one record. Assign four roles: commander, communications, resolver and

00:00:42.585 --> 00:00:43.385
<v Anna>scribe.

00:00:43.721 --> 00:00:45.277
<v Greg>Agree when the next update is due.

00:00:45.602 --> 00:00:48.353
<v Greg>Test the safest recovery path, verify that service has actually

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:51.569
<v Greg>returned, and hand off every remaining action with an owner.

00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:55.830
<v Anna>That's the major incident drill: one of seven narrated lessons

00:00:55.870 --> 00:00:57.670
<v Anna>in the Incident Triage Lab.

00:00:58.013 --> 00:01:01.008
<v Greg>Then it's your turn. Five tickets land in your queue.

00:01:01.333 --> 00:01:04.073
<v Greg>You classify each one, set its priority, assign an owner

00:01:04.155 --> 00:01:05.710
<v Greg>and choose the first customer update.

00:01:06.407 --> 00:01:08.880
<v Greg>Your score is reported to your learning management system.

00:01:09.286 --> 00:01:13.126
<v Anna>The Incident Triage Lab, from IT Professional Practice.

00:01:13.606 --> 00:01:16.246
<v Anna>Practise the response before you need it.
